A||N||15||Ozzy||General Fiero handling questions!||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||03-12-2000||03:53 PM||||Ok...I have an '88 Coupe. I have, so far, replaced LBJ's (Moog), installed Prothane bushings in the upper & lower control arms, replaced the worn steering rack bushings, installed Mc Pherson shocks, checked the wheel bearings (No problems), inner & outer tie rod ends etc, etc.<P>I have put some Goodyear Eagle HP's all around. 215/60/15's. I have done nothing to the rear end yet. I have new Mc Pherson struts to put on but have not done it yet. I have looked closely at the bushings and they look cracked around the outer edges. Stock for sure so I know theyt need replaced. Cradle??? I guess there is cradle bushing too. And I'm not able to see clearly enough but I think the front engine mount has a small crack in the rubber.<P><BR>I guess my question is what should this Fiero really feel like on the road? The front feels much improved and a lot more predictable but if you change direction in steering quickly, it seems to have a lot of sway. Is this where a rear sway bar and tighter bushings come in?? Is the after market steering damper for the '88 something worth installing? What are the esential elements related to steering and suspension on the '88 to make it like a "Sled on rails"?? Or, am I just expecting too much and that the '88 fresh off the assy. line was nice but not really that great?<P>Thnx,<P>Ozzy<P>P.S. other than these little things, I'm finally really happy with the way the Lil' beast is running. Can't wait to jump in and drive it!!! (Smile, Grin, Gloat etc. Gotta learn how to post those damn smilies soon!!)||||reg||5
Z||000001||kupop87gt||03-12-2000||05:22 PM||||Seems to me that tightening up the front end only would make it understeer more...||||reg||1
Z||000002||kupop87gt||03-12-2000||05:22 PM||||Seems to me that tightening up the front end only would make it understeer more...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric||03-12-2000||07:03 PM||||You may have gone too wide in front with the tires.  Wider tires seem to steer themselves with road variation, but 215 really isn't too bad.  You could get rid of the sway by installing stiffer springs and or stiffer shocks.  What kind of shocks do you have?  Did you replace the shocks or just the struts, I'm confused?<P>What kind of difference did you get with the new bushings?  Is the ride overly harsh now?  I'd like to go to poly bushings too, but people have had a lot of bad things to say about them.<P>My 88GT feels like a "sled on rails".||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-12-2000||07:46 PM||||A little bit of wear in the rear bushings and/or ball joints can cause more handling problems than some people realize. Also shocks and struts can make or break good handling. How about alignment?<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||03-12-2000||08:02 PM||||There are no bushings in the cradle so you don't need to replace those. <P>If I were in the same boat as you(hey, I am) I would get a rear swaybar for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ozzy||03-12-2000||09:41 PM||||Batboy...No I have not had a 4 wheel alignment yet. Was waiting untill I installed the new struts and possibly new bushings rear, then would get a 4 wheel alignment. The front upper & lower ball joints are all good.<P>SCCA... Yeah, I've been thinking about the rear sway bar for awhile. Hey, did your Coupe have the top of the wheel arches approx 3" above the top of the tyre?? I'm beginning to wonder if these springs are stock or modified. I saw this 85GT with Eibach springs yesterday that lowered the vehicle. It looked about the same height as mine!<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shiner||03-12-2000||10:27 PM||||You confused me a bit here, when you said McPhearson shocks.  There is no such beast.  <P> Go get the car aligned right now, or stop driving it!  Your tires are going to wear unevenly until you do.<P> Didn't all the '88s come with a rear bar stock?  Or was it just the GT and Formula?  I was under the impression that they all did.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ozzy||03-13-2000||12:06 AM||||Dude...I have had it aligned (2 Wheel) after the LBJ, Prothane bushings and shocks were done.<P>It may be out slightly but not that radicaly bad. And I have Mc Phearson shocks on the front!! Trust me on this!!!<P>And, NO the '88 Coupe did not have a rear sway bar.<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 03-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||03-13-2000||12:56 AM||||"Dude",<BR> Don't jump up my azz here.  Nowhere did you mention that you had the car aligned.  You said you were "waiting" to get a four wheel alignment.  <P> WTF is a McPhearson SHOCK then?  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||ChadMan||03-13-2000||09:27 AM||||Hey Ozzy, my 88 Coupe's suspension seemed really soft and willowy when I bought it.  A set of KYB shocks/struts helped a lot, but what made the biggest difference was replacing the rear springs with Formula springs.  This won't affect your ride height, but according to the Fiero Store, the Coupe springs are softer.<P>I thought about replacing the front springs, but why?  I'm happy with the way the car handles now!<P>And I have no rear sway bar.  I couldn't find one for an 88!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ozzy||03-13-2000||01:37 PM||||Shiner...settle down... not tryin' to jump yo at all. I assume that folks would consider me intelligent enough to get at least a 2 wheel alignment after tearing apart the front end. Relevance of a 4 wheel alignment to rear end work. (Maybe I'm wrong in this?)<P>I will try to dig out the receipts from the shocks and struts...<P><BR>Chadman, I will check out the ride when I get the new struts on and go from there. I suspect the rear may need stiffer springs.<P>Does anyone have an equivalent spring rate of the formula springs?<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000012||SCCA FIERO||03-13-2000||08:45 PM||||Ed Parks told me the Coupe and Formula springs have the same rate, but the Formula springs are longer. I don't know who is right.. I haven't had both springs to compare.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula||03-13-2000||10:32 PM||||my formula feels like its on rails, but it is a really bumpy track.<P>there is no sway whatsoever in my car, it doesnt even feel like its shifting weight around turns, but when it starts to cut lose it tells you to stop.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ozzy||03-14-2000||09:14 AM||||I'm going to check out an 86 SE this afternoon. I'm told it drives very well so maybe I'll have something to compare it too.<P>The 'shifting weight' description is accurate actually. It is not severe but enough to be a little un-nerving.<P>I would really like to get this car on a flat paved track somewhere to push it to it's limits so I can see or feel it's legs.<P>I can dig the bumpy scenerio too. After new shocks and Prothane bushings in the front end, I could feel a considerable change to the stiffness of the front suspension.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000015||Old Lar||03-14-2000||01:41 PM||||Just got my '87 GT back after new bushings were put in.  The car rides real firm and I'm on original suspension components.  I'm not hard on the car but there is a real difference from last year.  I'm hoping that they will soften up a little during my trip to Daytona next week. Twin Lakes did a nice job.  Now all I need to do is clean the car.  If the snow and cold weather goes away I can do stuff this weekend.||||reg||14
